A||||1||mbramble||Cooling Fan Problem||||1||
Z||000000||mbramble||08-18-2001||12:15 AM||||I have a strange problem in my 85GT.  It isn't on the road yet but I let it run for awhile every week or so to keep the battery charged, especially after I have spent all day working in it with the doors open or after using lots of juice testing out the new power windows, etc.  Anyway, a couple of weeks ago I found that the fans would not come on, even when I turned on the AC. I found that I had a bad AC/heater contol that wasn't passing the required ground thru to the fan relays when you set it to AC.  Replaced it with a spare and all seemed well (though the engine had not run long enough to know if the fans would come on by themselves after it got hot enough).  <P>Yesterday I had it running for awhile and it got HOT, the fans never came on and again would not come on with the AC. To make a long story short, if I unplug the engine compartment fan relay, after awhile the front one will kick on with the AC.  If I plug it back in, the front fan goes off and won't come back on immediately even if I unplug the rear fan relay again.  Once it does come back on, I can turn it on and off at will -- until I plug the rear relay back in.  When I measure the relay control voltage when things aren't working there is nothing there.  Once the front fan starts working again and I measure the control volate at both relays it is the full battery voltage.  <P>I checked the rear fan and relay by giving them power from an alternate 12V source and they work fine.  Could this be a strange ground (or lack thereof) problem??  This car had lots of funky wiring when I got it -- including having the battery moved to the trunk with a dual battery setup.  I removed that and moved the battery to the front.  I have yet to check to see if all the engine compartment grounds are in place and have clean connections.  Could this be the problem??  It is so strange for a voltage to just disappear!!  The fuse doesn't blow and as far as I can tell the only two things powered by the C/H fuse are the two fans.  It provides both relay control voltage and fan voltage for the rear fan and relay control voltage to the front fan.  Fan voltage for the front fan comes from a fusible link.<P>Any ideas other than checking all the grounds which I will do tomorrow??||||reg||1
